                'note' => 'Symmetrical flooding extends equally port and starboard; Unsymmetrical flooding (due to longitudinal bulkheads) causes severe list and must be equalized within 15 minutes using automatic cross-flooding ducts.',
                'topic' => 'Longitudinal Stability, Trim, Squat & Hull Stresses',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is a Damage Control Plan and Booklet?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A statutory diagram displayed on the bridge/CCR showing watertight subdivision boundaries, openings, closing appliances, and cross-flooding arrangements, accompanied by a booklet guiding the Master on damage stability recovery.',
                'topic' => 'Longitudinal Stability, Trim, Squat & Hull Stresses',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Define Hydrostatic Draft.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'The true mean draft measured at the Center of Flotation (LCF), which must be used when entering the Hydrostatic Particulars tables so trim distortions are eliminated.',
                'topic' => 'Longitudinal Stability, Trim, Squat & Hull Stresses',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Why does a vessel lose GM immediately upon grounding on a rock or shoal at a falling tide?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'The upward ground reaction force (P) at the keel acts exactly like discharging P tons from the keel (K), causing a virtual rise in the Center of Gravity (G) while the lost buoyancy lowers M.',
                'topic' => 'Longitudinal Stability, Trim, Squat & Hull Stresses',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'At what point during Dry Docking is a vessel at maximum risk of tipping or losing stability?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'The Critical Instant, which starts when the stern post first touches the blocks (if trimmed by the stern) and peaks just before the entire keel settles flat along the blocks.',
                'topic' => 'Longitudinal Stability, Trim, Squat & Hull Stresses',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Why is it preferable to enter a Dry Dock with minimal trim (less than 1% of length)?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A large stern trim builds up a massive upward force P at the aft keel block before the rest of the hull is supported by bilge shores/blocks, risking structural crushing of the stern frame or capsizing on the blocks.',
                'topic' => 'Longitudinal Stability, Trim, Squat & Hull Stresses',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How do you prepare a ship for Dry Docking?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'Minimize trim and ensure zero list, maximize positive GM, press up or completely empty tanks to eliminate free surface, secure all heavy gear, prepare the Docking Plan (showing plugs, transducers, sea chests), and record all tank soundings before entering.',
                'topic' => 'Longitudinal Stability, Trim, Squat & Hull Stresses',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Compare Transverse Framing, Longitudinal Framing, and Combined Framing.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'Transverse: closely spaced ribs/frames (good for general cargo ships); Longitudinal: closely spaced longitudinal stiffeners supported by wide web frames (resists hogging/sagging bending on long tankers and bulkers); Combined: longitudinal framing on deck and double bottom with transverse framing on side shells.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Define Garboard Strake, Sheer Strake, and Bilge Strake.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'Garboard Strake: the bottom shell plating strake immediately adjacent to the flat plate keel on either side; Sheer Strake: the uppermost strake of side shell plating meeting the strength deck; Bilge Strake: the curved plating joining the flat bottom to the vertical side shell.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Define Camber, Sheer, Tumblehome, Flare, and Rise of Floor.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'Camber: transverse upward arch of the weather deck to shed water; Sheer: longitudinal upward curve of the deck toward bow and stern (adds reserve buoyancy); Tumblehome: inward slope of upper side shell; Flare: outward curvature of the forward bow above the waterline; Rise of Floor: slight upward slope of the flat bottom from keel to bilge.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What structural arrangements at the forward end resist Panting?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Panting stringers (horizontal side girders spaced every ~2m vertically), panting beams tied across the forepeak, perforated wash plates, and 15–20% thicker shell plating forward of the collision bulkhead.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What structural arrangements at the forward bottom resist Pounding (Slamming)?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Solid floors on every frame space in the pounding region (between 0.05L and 0.25L–0.30L from FP), additional longitudinal side girders spaced closer together, and thickened bottom shell plating.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Where must the Collision Bulkhead be located on a cargo ship?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Watertight from double bottom to the freeboard deck, located between 0.05L and 0.08L (or 0.05L+3 m, whichever is less) abaft the Forward Perpendicular (FP).',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Can any doors or manholes be cut into the Collision Bulkhead below the freeboard deck?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'No doors, manholes, or access openings are permitted; only one single pipe may pierce it to service the forepeak tank, fitted with a screw-down valve on the collision bulkhead operable from above the freeboard deck.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the minimum number of Transverse Watertight Bulkheads on a conventional cargo ship?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'At least four: 1) Collision Bulkhead, 2) Forward Engine Room Bulkhead, 3) Aft Engine Room (After Peak) Bulkhead, plus intermediate hold bulkheads according to ship length.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Compare a Solid Floor, Plate (Bracket) Floor, and Watertight Floor in a Double Bottom.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'Solid Floor: a continuous transverse plate with lightening holes and manholes plus stiffeners; Bracket Floor: open frame made of top frame bar and bottom reverse frame connected only by end brackets at the center girder and margin plate; Watertight Floor: solid unperforated plate separating two different double-bottom tanks.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is a Duct Keel and where is it fitted?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'A hollow watertight box tunnel running along the centerline inside the double bottom from the forward engine room bulkhead to the forward cargo hold, housing bilge/ballast/bunker pipelines so pipes don\'t pass through cargo holds and remain accessible at sea.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What safety precautions apply before entering a Duct Keel?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Treat it strictly as a high-risk Enclosed Space: complete an Enclosed Space Entry Permit, ventilate mechanically, test atmosphere (O₂, HC, toxic gases), verify lighting, and check for pipeline leaks.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is a Bilge Keel and why is it connected to the hull via a Doubler Plate (Flat Bar)?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'A long fin projecting from the turn of the bilge over the midship half-length to dampen rolling; welded to a flat doubling strip rather than directly to the bilge strake so that if the bilge keel is torn off by a tug or berth, it rips the weld off the doubler without tearing a hole in the hull shell plating.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is a Coffin Plate and a Shoe Plate at the sternframe?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'The Coffin Plate is the U-shaped shell plate wrapping around the aft bossing/sternframe; the Shoe Plate connects the bottom of the sternframe across to the keel plate.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the Rudder Carrier Bearing and Pintle Jumping Clearance?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'The Rudder Carrier Bearing inside the steering flat supports the vertical weight of the rudder stock and blade; a Jumping Stopper is fitted under the rudder horn with ~2–3 mm clearance to prevent the rudder pintles from jumping out of their gudgeons in heavy seas.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How is a Rudder Blade tested for watertightness in Dry Dock?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Compressed air at slight pressure (~0.2 bar) is applied through the top plug while soapy water is brushed along welds, or the rudder is filled with water/kerosene to check for weeping cracks (empty via the bottom drain plug before undocking).',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are Sacrificial Anodes vs. Impressed Current Cathodic Protection (ICCP)?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'Sacrificial Anodes are blocks of Zinc or Aluminum welded to the underwater hull/ballast tanks that corrode preferentially to steel; ICCP uses hull reference electrodes and inert titanium/platinum anodes supplied with low-voltage DC current to suppress galvanic hull corrosion.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Why must Zinc Anodes never be fitted inside Oil Tanker Cargo Tanks?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'If a heavy zinc anode detaches and falls onto rusty steel at the bottom of a tank, the impact creates an incendive spark; Aluminum anodes face strict drop-energy limits (<275 Joules) inside tanks.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Why is the ICCP system switched off when an Oil Tanker berths at a terminal?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'To avoid stray electrical potential differences between the ship\'s hull and the shore jetty structure during hose connection.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is a Corrugated Bulkhead and what are its advantages?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A bulkhead pressed with vertical or horizontal trapezoidal troughs instead of welded stiffeners; eliminates stiffener brackets, reduces structural weight for the same strength, and makes tank/hold washing much faster on chemical tankers and bulkers.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are Upper and Lower Stools on a Bulk Carrier transverse bulkhead?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Box-shaped sloped structures at the top and bottom of a vertically corrugated cargo hold bulkhead; the lower stool sheds bulk cargo toward the center for grabs and absorbs huge bending moments at the bulkhead base.',
                'topic' => 'Ship Construction, Framing & Structural Members',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the dimensions of the Load Line Disc (Plimsoll Mark) and Deck Line?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Deck Line: 300 mm×25 mm painted at the freeboard deck level; Load Line Ring: 300 mm outside diameter (25 mm thick) intersected by a 450 mm×25 mm horizontal line whose upper edge passes through the center of the ring (Summer draft).',
                'topic' => 'Dry Docking, Load Lines & Watertight Integrity',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is stability?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'The ability of a ship to return to her original upright position after the external force that heeled her has been removed.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How is KM obtained, and what is BM?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'KM = KB + BM, where BM = I/V (second moment of area of the waterplane about the centreline ÷ underwater volume). KM is read from the hydrostatic curves for the draught.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'On what does the free surface effect depend?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'On the length × breadth³ of the free surface and the density of the liquid; it is independent of the quantity of liquid in a slack tank and of the tank\'s height. Halving the breadth reduces the effect to one-eighth.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the approximate formula for the rolling period?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'T ≈ 0.8 × B / √GM (B = breadth). A longer rolling period means a smaller GM, so the period measured on board gives a quick check of GM.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is synchronous rolling and how do you stop it?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'It occurs when the wave encounter period equals the ship\'s natural rolling period, so the roll amplitude builds dangerously. Alter course and/or speed to change the encounter period.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is parametric rolling?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'Roll caused by cyclic changes of waterplane shape (and so of GM) as waves pass, mainly in head or following seas when the encounter period is about half the natural roll period. Container ships with flared bows and wide sterns are especially at risk. Alter course or speed.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the effect on GM of adding weight above or below G?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Weight added above G raises G and reduces GM; weight added below G lowers G and increases GM.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What precautions are needed for a heavy lift?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Check GM and free surface before the lift (G effectively moves to the point of suspension), secure the boom/crane with guys, ballast to limit heel, and stop in adverse weather or swell.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the angle of deck-edge immersion?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'The angle at which the deck edge enters the water. The GZ curve normally has a point of inflection here.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the angle of vanishing stability?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'The angle at which GZ returns to zero on the GZ curve; beyond it the ship will capsize.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the Weather Criterion?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A check that the ship can withstand a steady beam wind combined with a gust and a rolling motion in waves, using heeling levers compared with the GZ curve (2008 IS Code).',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Why must the stability booklet be kept on board?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'It contains the approved loading conditions, hydrostatic data and KN curves. The Master must check stability before sailing and compare the loaded condition with the approved ones.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is a lightweight survey and how often is it done?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A survey to verify the lightship weight and centre of gravity, at intervals not exceeding 5 years. If the deviation exceeds about 2% of lightship displacement (or 1% of L in LCG), re-inclining is required.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => true,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the main steps of a draught survey?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Read the six draughts, correct for trim, hog/sag and density, find displacement from hydrostatics, deduct ballast, fuel, fresh water and stores, deduct lightship and the constant, and the remainder is the cargo.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What precautions apply when carrying timber deck cargo?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Stow and lash to the Timber Code, allow for absorbed water (about 10%) in stability calculations, keep a positive GM throughout, and keep a walkway/lifelines for the crew.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How does ice accretion affect stability?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'It adds weight high up, so G rises and GM falls, and increases the windage area. Remove the ice promptly and alter course to reduce spray.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How does consuming bunkers affect stability?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Weight is removed from low tanks so G rises and GM reduces; free surface effects also appear in partially empty tanks. Check stability for the arrival condition before sailing.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How does high deck cargo or high container stacking affect stability?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'G rises and GM falls, and windage and lashing loads increase. Check against the approved loading conditions and the cargo securing manual.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Why is a satisfactory GM alone not a guarantee of safety?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'The GZ curve may still have a small range or low maximum GZ. Always check the full IS Code criteria, not only the initial GM.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Stability, Rolling & Loading Questions',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the types of keel?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Flat plate keel (the most common), bar keel, and duct keel (pipe tunnel).',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the purposes of the double bottom?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'Protection against bottom damage, carriage of ballast/fuel/fresh water, trimming and heeling control, and a strong base for the hull girder. SOLAS II-1 Reg 9 requires double bottoms on most cargo ships of 500 GT and over (tankers have separate MARPOL requirements).',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What precautions apply to watertight doors?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Keep them closed at sea, check local and remote operation and the bridge indicators, and test them during drills.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the types of rudder?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Unbalanced, balanced and semi-balanced; spade rudders; and rudders with a skeg or horn (e.g. mariner type).',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are hogging and sagging?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Hogging: the ship is supported at the middle by a wave crest, so the deck is in tension and the keel in compression. Sagging: the ship is supported at the ends by crests, so the deck is in compression and the keel in tension.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What types of corrosion occur on ships?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Uniform (general), galvanic (dissimilar metals), pitting, crevice, and erosion/abrasion corrosion.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the PSPC?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'The IMO Performance Standard for Protective Coatings for ballast tanks (SOLAS II-1 Reg 3-2), with a target coating life of 15 years.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are freeing ports?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Openings in the bulwarks to clear water from the deck quickly. They must never be blocked or fitted with unapproved closures.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What must be done with ventilators and air pipes in heavy weather?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Their weathertight closing appliances must be kept closed to preserve weathertight integrity.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the purpose of a sounding pipe?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'To allow tanks and bilges to be sounded. They are fitted with self-closing caps or cocks where required.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the purpose of a bulbous bow?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'It reduces wave-making resistance and improves fuel economy near the design speed. Take care with anchors and in berthing.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the checks on the stern tube seal?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Check for leakage at the forward and aft seals, the lubricating oil level and temperature, and the oil sample for water.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is a classification society and what is IACS?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A recognised organisation that sets and verifies rules for hull and machinery construction and maintenance. IACS is the International Association of Classification Societies.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What types of survey does a cargo ship undergo?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Initial, annual, intermediate, special (renewal, every 5 years), dry-dock/bottom survey, and for bulk carriers and tankers the Enhanced Survey Programme (ESP).',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the dry-docking interval?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Two bottom inspections in any 5 years, with no more than 36 months between. Check flag state requirements.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => true,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is inspected while the ship is in dry dock?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Hull plating and coating, rudder and bearings, propeller and tailshaft clearances, sea chests and valves, anodes, bilge keels, anchors and cables.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the statutory certificates of a cargo ship and their validity?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Cargo Ship Safety Construction, Safety Equipment, Safety Radio, Load Line, IOPP, ISPP, IAPP, ISSC, SMC and MLC certificates. Most are valid for 5 years with annual/intermediate endorsements.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the parts of an anchor cable?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Shackles of cable, joining shackles, end links and a bitter end secured in the chain locker. A standard shackle is 27.5 m (15 fathoms).',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the types of anchor?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Stockless anchors, and HHP and SHHP (high and super high holding power) anchors, which have more holding power for the same weight.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the Equipment Number?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A number calculated from the ship\'s dimensions and windage area that determines the number, weight and size of anchors, chain and mooring ropes from the class rules.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What do you check when inspecting anchor chain?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Reduction in link diameter (replace at about 12% wear), stud looseness, cracks, shackle pins, and the condition of the bitter end.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the main mooring safety points?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Know the SWL of lines, keep clear of the snap-back zone, check winch brakes and bollards, and do not mix rope types of different elasticity in one lead.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the SOLAS requirements for steering gear?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'The main steering gear must put the rudder from 35° one side to 35° the other at maximum service speed, and 35° to 30° within 28 seconds. The auxiliary gear must go 15° to 15° at half speed or 7 knots (whichever is greater) within 60 seconds. Test before departure (within 12 hours) and hold emergency steering drills every 3 months.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the procedure for entering an enclosed space?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Permit to work, ventilate and test the atmosphere (O₂ 20.9%, flammable gas below 1% LEL, toxic gases), standby person, rescue equipment ready and communications maintained.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What precautions apply to a bow thruster?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Check the power supply, warn people near the hull, check the tunnel is clear, and use only within the speed limits.',
                'topic' => 'Additional Ship Construction, Equipment & Surveys',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the purpose of the Load Line Convention (1966)?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'It sets minimum freeboard to ensure adequate reserve buoyancy and watertight integrity for the ship.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Define freeboard.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'The distance from the upper edge of the deck line to the upper edge of the load line mark.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the load line marks?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'TF (Tropical Fresh), F (Fresh), T (Tropical), S (Summer), W (Winter) and WNA (Winter North Atlantic). Timber ships have the additional lumber marks.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How are the Winter, Tropical and Fresh marks positioned?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Winter is 1/48 of the moulded summer draught below S; Tropical is 1/48 above S; Fresh Water is above the corresponding mark by the Fresh Water Allowance.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are Type A and Type B ships?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Type A ships carry liquid cargoes in bulk and have high integrity (lowest freeboard). Type B is all other ships.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the main conditions of assignment?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Structural strength and stability information, weathertight and watertight integrity of hatchways, doors, ventilators and air pipes, freeing ports, guard rails, and protection of the crew.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the load line surveys?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Initial, annual (within 3 months either side of the anniversary date) and renewal survey at 5-year intervals.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Define gross tonnage and net tonnage.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'GT is a measure of the total enclosed volume of the ship (GT = K₁V). NT represents the cargo-carrying capacity and cannot be less than 0.30 GT.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Define displacement, lightship and deadweight.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Displacement is the total weight of the ship; lightship is the weight of the hull, machinery and equipment; deadweight = displacement − lightship (cargo, fuel, water, stores, crew).',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What must the Master do if the load line is submerged?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'He must not sail with the load line submerged; reduce the load or ballast until the correct line for the zone and season is at the waterline.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Which tonnage is used for port dues?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Normally GT or NT, depending on the port tariff.',
                'topic' => 'Load Lines, Freeboard & Tonnage',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Explain the fire triangle and tetrahedron.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'The fire triangle: fuel, oxygen and heat. The tetrahedron adds the chemical chain reaction.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the classes of fire?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'A: solids; B: flammable liquids; C: gases; D: metals; F: cooking oils and fats.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the methods of extinguishing a fire?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Starving (removing fuel), smothering (removing oxygen), cooling, and interrupting the chain reaction.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Name the extinguishing agents and their uses.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Water (class A), foam (class B), CO₂ (electrical and class B), dry powder (B, C and electrical), wet chemical (class F). Halon is banned.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Why is water not used on electrical or oil fires?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Water conducts electricity, and on burning oil it can spread the fire.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the CO2 quantities for fixed fire-extinguishing systems?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Cargo spaces: 30% of the gross volume of the largest cargo space; machinery spaces: 40% of the gross volume (35% if the casing volume is excluded).',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => true,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Describe the CO2 release procedure for the engine room.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 120,
                'note' => 'Muster and count the crew, stop machinery and fuel supply, close ventilators and openings, sound the pre-discharge alarm, open the release cabinet and operate the pilot cylinders, then release the main CO₂. Keep the space closed and cool the boundaries before ventilating.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Why is there a time delay before CO2 release?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'To allow personnel to leave the space. An audible and visual alarm operates during the delay.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the dangers of CO2?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'It causes asphyxiation and cold burns, and is heavier than air so it collects in low spaces.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Name the types of portable extinguisher and their use.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Water, foam, dry powder (ABC), CO₂ and wet chemical, each matched to the class of fire.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the SOLAS requirements for fire pumps?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Cargo ships of 1000 GT and above: at least two independent power-driven pumps; an emergency fire pump on ships of 2000 GT and over; minimum hydrant pressure 0.27 N/mm² (6000 GT and over) or 0.25 N/mm² (below 6000 GT).',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => true,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the hose and nozzle requirements?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Each hydrant must have a hose, a dual-purpose (jet/spray) nozzle and a spanner. Hose length at least 10 m; maximum 15 m in machinery spaces, 20 m on other decks, and 25 m on open decks with a breadth over 30 m.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => true,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What does a fireman\'s outfit contain?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Protective clothing, boots, gloves, helmet, electric safety lamp (3 hours), axe, SCBA (about 30 minutes) and a fireproof lifeline. At least two outfits are carried.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is an EEBD?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'An emergency escape breathing device giving at least 10 minutes of air for escaping from a space with smoke or toxic gas.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How does the fire detection and alarm system work?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Smoke, heat and flame detectors and manual call points feed an indicator panel on the bridge. The system is tested regularly.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Explain A, B and C class divisions.',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A class: steel or equivalent, resisting the passage of smoke and flame for 60, 30 or 0 minutes (A-60, A-30, A-0). B class: non-combustible panels, 30 or 15 minutes. C class: non-combustible materials.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the fire control plan?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A plan permanently exhibited showing control stations, fire sections, detection and extinguishing systems and means of access. A copy is kept in a prominently marked weathertight container outside the deckhouse for shore fire-fighters.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How often are fire drills held?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'At least monthly, and each crew member should take part in at least one fire drill and one abandon ship drill each month. If over 25% of the crew have not taken part in a drill in the previous month, hold one within 24 hours of leaving port.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is your first action when you discover a fire?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Raise the alarm, report to the bridge giving the location and nature of the fire, and fight the fire only if it is safe to do so.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the immediate actions for an engine room fire?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Raise the alarm, shut the fuel quick-closing valves, stop ventilation, boundary cool, evacuate and account for all personnel, and release the fixed system if necessary.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How do you deal with a fire in a cargo hold?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Stop ventilation, close the hatch, use the fixed CO₂, high expansion foam or inert gas system, and monitor temperatures. Do not open the hold until the fire is out.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How do you deal with a galley fire?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Switch off the power/gas, smother with a fire blanket or wet chemical extinguisher, and never use water on cooking oil.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'Which cargoes and materials are prone to spontaneous combustion?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Oily rags, coal, DRI, fishmeal, copra and wet cotton. Ventilate and monitor temperatures.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is required for hot work?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A hot work permit, a gas-free test, removal of combustibles, fire protection and fire watch ready, and authority from the Master or Chief Officer.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is boundary cooling and what must you watch?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Cooling the bulkheads and decks around a fire to stop it spreading. Beware of the free surface effect from the fire-fighting water.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the oxygen limit for an inert gas system, and which tankers need one?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Oxygen not more than 8% by volume, with a positive pressure in the tanks. Required for oil tankers of 20,000 DWT and over.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => true,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are low and high expansion foam used for?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Low expansion foam (up to about 12:1) for deck and tank fires; high expansion foam (over 200:1) for engine rooms and cargo holds.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How are smoke and fire spread controlled?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Close fire dampers, doors and ventilation to isolate the space and keep smoke from spreading.',
                'topic' => 'Fire Prevention & Fire-Fighting (SOLAS II-2)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the LSA Code?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'The international code setting out the technical requirements for the life-saving appliances required by SOLAS Chapter III.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the abandon ship/ general emergency alarm signal?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Seven or more short blasts followed by one long blast on the whistle or alarm bell.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What does the muster list contain?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'The emergency signals, the duties of each crew member, and substitutes for key personnel. It is posted in conspicuous places.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the lifeboat capacity requirements on a cargo ship?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Lifeboats on each side with a total capacity of 100% of persons on board per side, or a free-fall lifeboat at the stern for 100% plus liferafts. Check SOLAS III/31 for the exact arrangement.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => true,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the equipment of a lifeboat?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Oars, buoyant lifeline, bailers, sea anchor, compass, rations (10,000 kJ per person), fresh water (3 litres per person), first-aid kit, fishing kit, flares, torch, whistle and radar reflector.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the weekly and monthly lifeboat checks?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Weekly: visual inspection and run the engine for at least 3 minutes ahead and astern. Monthly: check all equipment, swing out the davits, and inspect the lifejackets and immersion suits.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How often are lifeboats launched?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Lowered into the water with the assigned crew at least once every 3 months; free-fall lifeboats are launched at least once every 6 months.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => true,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the requirements for lifejackets?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'One for every person on board plus extras at watch stations. A lifejacket light of at least 0.75 cd for 8 hours, a whistle and retro-reflective tape.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What performance tests must a lifejacket pass?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'It can be donned in 1 minute, worn after a jump from 4.5 m, and turns an unconscious person face up within a few seconds.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the difference between an immersion suit and a thermal protective aid?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'An immersion suit protects against hypothermia in cold water and has buoyancy; a TPA (thermal protective aid) is a bag-type aid for persons without suits.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How many lifebuoys must a cargo ship carry?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'By length: under 100 m = 8; 100–150 m = 10; 150–200 m = 12; 200 m and over = 14. Half have self-igniting lights, two have a light and smoke signal, and at least one on each side has a buoyant lifeline.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => true,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What markings are required on a lifebuoy?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'The ship\'s name and port of registry in block capitals, and retro-reflective material.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the types and characteristics of pyrotechnics?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Parachute flares reach about 300 m and burn about 40 seconds; hand flares burn for about 1 minute; buoyant smoke signals last about 3 minutes. The bridge carries 12 parachute flares.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is the line-throwing appliance requirement?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Range at least 230 m in calm weather, with 4 rockets and 4 lines.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is an EPIRB and how is it tested?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A 406 MHz float-free emergency beacon with a hydrostatic release and a battery life of at least 48 hours. Test monthly using the self-test function; the HRU is replaced every 2 years and the battery at expiry.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What is a SART and how many are required?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'A 9 GHz radar transponder that shows 12 dots on the interrogating radar. Standby of 96 hours and 8 hours transmitting. Cargo ships of 500 GT and over carry at least 2.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How many two-way VHF radiotelephones are carried?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 60,
                'note' => 'Three on ships of 500 GT and over, and two on ships of 300 to 500 GT.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the main details of a liferaft?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Inflates within 1 minute, painter of 15 m with a weak link, hydrostatic release unit, and annual servicing at an approved station.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'How do you launch a throw-over liferaft?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
                'note' => 'Check the painter is secured to the ship, throw the raft overboard, pull the painter to inflate it, and board directly from the ship if possible.',
                'topic' => 'Life-Saving Appliances & Abandon Ship (SOLAS III)',
                'needs_review' => false,
            ],
            [
                'text' => 'What are the requirements for a rescue boat?',
                'category' => 'technical',
                'duration_seconds' => 90,
